-- title:
--   Equation (15) — general Euclidean stochastic projected-subgradient step
-- statement:
--   Let $S\subseteq\mathbb R^n$ be compact and convex, let $\Phi$ be real and convex on $S$, and let $g(z)$ be a subgradient at every $z\in S$, so $\Phi(z)+\langle g(z),v-z\rangle\le\Phi(v)$ for $z,v\in S$. On a probability space let $w,H$ be measurable random vectors, with $w\in S$ almost surely, $\|H\|\le B$ almost surely for a finite $B\ge0$, and $\mathbb E[H\mid w]=g(w)$ almost surely. For any deterministic $\epsilon>0$ and any comparator $u\in S$, put $w'=P_S(w-\epsilon H)$. Then all four integrands below are integrable and
--
--   $$\mathbb E[\Phi(w)-\Phi(u)]\le\frac{\mathbb E\|w-u\|^2}{2\epsilon}-\frac{\mathbb E\|w'-u\|^2}{2\epsilon}+\frac\epsilon2\mathbb E\|H\|^2.$$
--
--   This is the full finite-dimensional one-step estimate of Appendix A. It can be applied separately on each growing inventory interval once the comparator belongs to that interval. Membership of $u$ makes $S$ nonempty; $n=0$ is harmless. There is no differentiability or minimization hypothesis.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: the particular stepsize of Lemma 3 and its minimizing comparator → any deterministic positive one-step size and any comparator in the same compact convex set; reason: the source case is obtained by taking its schedule and its minimizer; a new set may be supplied at each application
--   - encoding: random directions H(w_t) conditioned on the current iterate → a measurable current point w and a measurable bounded random vector H, with conditional expectation on the sigma algebra generated by w; reason: this is the source probabilistic convention and imposes no smoothness or loss bound
--   - generalization: the positive oracle bound implicit in the scheduled algorithm → a nonnegative oracle bound including zero; reason: the one-step formula divides only by the positive stepsize and remains valid for zero directions
--   - encoding: the finite real expectations in the displayed estimate → integrability of all four displayed integrands is also concluded; reason: compactness, bounded directions, the supporting subgradients and conditional unbiasedness supply integrability; it is not assumed
-- source:
--   Huh & Rusmevichientong (2009), A Nonparametric Asymptotic Analysis of Inventory Planning with Censored Demand, author’s manuscript associated with doi:10.1287/moor.1080.0355, https://doi.org/10.1287/moor.1080.0355, Appendix A, proof of Lemma 3, Equation (15), printed p. 16 / PDF p. 16; standing Euclidean assumptions printed p. 8 / PDF p. 8.

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_AIMInventory_Known_SGD

open MeasureTheory
open scoped RealInnerProductSpace

namespace AIMInventoryAudit.UnknownBound

/-- Appendix A, Equation (15), p. 16. Arbitrary positive one-step size and comparator.
No differentiability is required; unbiasedness is conditional on the current point. -/
theorem eq_15_full {n : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω]
    (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
    (S : Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))) (hS : IsCompact S) (hSconv : Convex ℝ S)
    (Φ : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → ℝ) (hΦ : ConvexOn ℝ S Φ)
    (g : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))
    (hg : ∀ z ∈ S, ∀ u ∈ S, Φ z + inner ℝ (g z) (u - z) ≤ Φ u)
    (w H : Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))
    (hwmeas : Measurable w) (hHmeas : Measurable H)
    (hw : ∀ᵐ ω ∂μ, w ω ∈ S)
    (B : ℝ) (hB : 0 ≤ B) (hbound : ∀ᵐ ω ∂μ, ‖H ω‖ ≤ B)
    (hunbiased : μ[H | MeasurableSpace.comap w inferInstance] =ᵐ[μ] fun ω => g (w ω))
    (ε : ℝ) (hε : 0 < ε) (u : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (hu : u ∈ S) :
    let wnext := fun ω => AIMInventory.Known.projSet S (w ω - ε • H ω)
    Integrable (fun ω => Φ (w ω) - Φ u) μ ∧
    Integrable (fun ω => ‖w ω - u‖ ^ 2) μ ∧
    Integrable (fun ω => ‖wnext ω - u‖ ^ 2) μ ∧
    Integrable (fun ω => ‖H ω‖ ^ 2) μ ∧
    (∫ ω, (Φ (w ω) - Φ u) ∂μ) ≤
      (∫ ω, ‖w ω - u‖ ^ 2 ∂μ) / (2 * ε) -
      (∫ ω, ‖wnext ω - u‖ ^ 2 ∂μ) / (2 * ε) +
      ε / 2 * (∫ ω, ‖H ω‖ ^ 2 ∂μ) := by sorry

end AIMInventoryAudit.UnknownBound
